{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-348024",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot (1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-(0)}}{2}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
